%N A401467 Variation of A400185: the same rules apply except that poles can only see each other if the direct line of sight between their tops is strictly above the tops of all other poles that lie directly on the same line of sight.
%C A401467 In this sequence, unlike A400185, for the poles to be visible the direct line of sight between their tops must be strictly above the tops of any other poles directly on the same line of sight. So, for example, if there were three poles in a row of height 3, 3, 4, and we placed a pole of height 3 to the left of these, then that new pole could only see TWO poles - the pole of height 3 adjacent to it, and the pole of height 4. It does not see the existing middle pole of height 3 as the line of sight between that pole and the new pole lies directly on top of the existing left pole of height 3. Similarly if there were poles of height 1, 2, 3, 4, then the pole of height 4 only sees one pole, that of height 3, as the tops of the other two are not above the line of sight between the poles of height 4 and 3. See A400185 for further details.
%C A401467 This variation allows multiple poles of the same height to be adjacent. In the first 10000 terms the longest run of poles of the same height is 8 poles of height 111, from a(112) to a(119). It is unknown if longer runs exist.
%C A401467 Note if this rule was applied to a single straight row of poles, as in A400000, then the sequence would simply be 0, 1, 1, 1, ... , with 1's repeating forever.
%H A401467 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A401467/b401467.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%e A401467 The spiral begins:
